-- title:
--   The sparse algorithm equals the dense one (round six, `segInvS_eq`)
-- statement:
--   **The sparse algorithm equals the dense one** under the hypotheses of `segInv_err`.
--
--   The following holds (Lean statement, in the scope of module `Solutions.J6SegSparse`):
--
--   ```lean
--   theorem J6Seg.segInvS_eq {E Dm Bu : ℝ} (hs : 0 < s) (hst : s ≤ t) (hα : 2 ≤ α) (hx : 3 ≤ xθ s t α)
--       (hB : BlocksOK s t B) (hlam : ∀ i < s, B.len i ≤ lam)
--       (hT : TabOK P s t α B T E Dm) (hE : 0 ≤ E) (hBu : 0 ≤ Bu)
--       (hP : 1000 * segG B lam E Dm Bu ≤ 2 ^ P) (U : ℕ → ℤ) :
--       ∀ i, segInvS P lam T B U i = segInv P T B U i
--   ```
--
--   **Context.** Segmented inverse (S2): the sparse algorithm
--
--   `segInvS P lam T B U` is `segInv` with the capacitance solved by `geSolveS` on the pattern
--   `bandS (λ + w) (s - λ - w)`: a band of half-width `λ + w` (blocks of length `≤ λ`, coupling width
--   `w`) plus the border of positions `≥ s - λ - w` (the cyclic wrap). Per row of the capacitance
--   at most `2(λ+w)+1` band entries plus the border are touched.
--
--   * `capI_band`: the capacitance vanishes off the pattern;
--   * `segInvS_eq`: under the hypotheses of `segInv_err`, `segInvS = segInv`;
--   * `segInvFullS_err`: the fully instantiated sparse algorithm satisfies the bound of
--     `segInvFull_err`.
